  "claim_contract": {
    "proved": "For a coordinate-regular spatially varying abelian connection, exact coordinate-edge interval integrals generate U(1) links whose ordered half-edge products equal the full edge exactly.  Uniform bounds on the six cross-coordinate derivatives imply quadratic bounds for the three plaquette phases and therefore for the five-face cubical gluing budget. The actual connection-generated finite-site link triples have zero straight-edge budget, while the complete finite-Hilbert edge-plus-gluing defect is bounded by h^2 times an explicit derivative envelope.  On a dyadic mesh these operator defects decay with ratio 1/4, are summable, and instantiate the checked global-limit theorem.  A non-affine witness A_y(x)=sin(x), with varying curvature F_xy(x)=cos(x), is included.",
    "not_proved": "The theorem assumes explicit one-dimensional differentiability and global bounds for the six cross-coordinate derivatives.  It gives a sufficient derivative-sum plaquette bound, not an exact general Stokes flux formula or a sharp bound solely by the curl.  It does not construct reconstruction into L2(R^3,C^4), identify the limit with the continuum Dirac propagator, or provide dynamical Maxwell/Yang-Mills equations.  No emergent geometry, gravity, cosmology, or empirical prediction is established."
  },
